# WHAT A GARDEN RECEIVES FROM A RELEASE — one pattern per line, relative to the release root.
# Read by seed/germinate.sh (a new garden) and bin/dmupgrade.py (an existing one), so the two can never
# disagree about what the language is. Patterns, not a file list: a hand-kept list once missed `dmsafe`,
# which `dmcheck` imports without saying so, and a pattern carries a new tool without anyone remembering to.
#
# The tools: every `bin/dm*.py`, the one installer and the hooks — and NOTHING ELSE under bin/. Copying the
# whole of bin/ once shipped one estate's private tools, host names and a LAN address into a new garden.
bin/dm*.py
bin/install.sh
bin/install.py
bin/hooks/*
# The law, the seed's templates, this file and the germination procedure.
seed/*
# The knowledge the vocabulary points at (9.1): published classifications and the technology catalogue, each file
# under its own terms (seed/knowledge/SOURCES.md). A garden that does not extend the `knowledge` profile never reads them.
seed/knowledge/*
# What the law MEANS and how a write is made. A garden grown without these had the law's data and no
# statement of what it meant; its first person bean took three attempts. The manifesto stands above them: the law
# names the clause it carries, and the name must open in every garden, offline.
MANIFESTO.md
MODEL.md
CHECKLIST.md
MERGE.md
# The semantic merge and the journal's union merge (.gitattributes), and no bytecode in any commit (.gitignore).
.gitattributes
.gitignore
# The skill a coding agent auto-loads: a mirror pointing into the garden, carrying no rule of its own.
AGENTS.md
.claude/skills/daftar/SKILL.md
# The pre-commit hook runs it, so a garden without it cannot commit. The other tests assert things about the
# release's own fixtures and stay in the release.
test/fast.py
